"According to the last paragraph, how does the metaphor change the question asked about illness?": "85efe6c13131",
"According to the passage, what government role is the heart given in the Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic, and what role is the lung given?": "583ff53940a6",
"According to the passage, why is \"the general\" a fitting title for the liver?": "7f97b13abf07",
"The Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic organizes the entire set of organs covered in this unit using a single extended metaphor: a royal court, with each organ holding a specific government post. The heart is named \"the ruler,\" responsible for the overall direction of the whole body, the way an emperor sets the direction of a kingdom without personally performing every task. The lung is named \"the prime minister,\" managing the flow of qi throughout the body the way a minister manages the flow of resources and orders through a government.": "07c272d122ec",
"The Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic organizes the entire set of organs covered in this unit using a single extended metaphor: a royal court, with each organ holding a specific government post. The heart is named \"the ruler,\" responsible for the overall direction of the whole body, the way an emperor sets the direction of a kingdom without personally performing every task. The lung is named \"the prime minister,\" managing the flow of qi throughout the body the way a minister manages the flow of resources and orders through a government. The liver is named \"the general,\" in charge of planning and strategy — a fitting role for the organ this course has already connected to smooth, forward-moving qi and to anger when that movement is blocked. The spleen and stomach are named officials in charge of granaries and storage, responsible for the intake and processing that feeds everyone else in the \"government.\" Each title isn't decoration; it's a compressed description of the organ's role relative to all the others. The real value of the metaphor is what it says about failure. A kingdom doesn't collapse because one official had a single bad day — it collapses when officials stop coordinating, when the ruler's direction isn't being carried out, or when one office starts overreaching into another's duties. Reading illness this way shifts the question from \"which single part is broken\" to \"where has the coordination between offices broken down\" — the same systems-level thinking this entire unit has been building toward.": "27c9fde49acf",
